\section{Analytical Observability, 21-State UES, and Local ISS}
\label{app:iss-stability}

This section establishes local stability of the normal Live-state linearization
with a slow Gauss--Markov accelerometer-bias residual.  Finite-horizon
observability of the translational and attitude/gyro-bias subsystems and
contraction of the residual-bias dynamics yield uniform detectability of the
21-state model.  With bounded Riccati covariance, the homogeneous error
recursion is UES; a local ISS bound then follows from the quadratic EKF
remainder.  Startup, hard resets, re-lock logic, arbitrary rejection patterns.
The default prediction-time PSD
covariance inflation remains within the result as a bounded positive-semidefinite
addition to process covariance.

\subsection{Exogenous scheduling and proof configuration}
\label{app:iss-tuner}

The adaptive wave variables are exogenous signals,
\begin{equation}
\vartheta(t)=\bigl(\tau(t),\sigma_{aw}(t),r_S(t)\bigr).
\label{eq:iss-schedule}
\end{equation}
The wave-period and variance estimators use raw IMU measurements and the
Mahony solution of Eqs.~\eqref{eq:proxy-mahony}--%
\eqref{eq:proxy-vertical}, which never reads OU--III state or covariance; the
information flow is therefore proxy~$\to$~MEKF only.  The same proxy supplies
startup attitude, magnetometer gating, and the magnetic-reference frame
(Sec.~\ref{sec:startup-policy}).  The initial handoff and the second magnetic
acquisition at $t=\SI{90}{s}$ are hard reconfiguration events and define Live
interval boundaries.

The online schedule is also predictable with respect to the current innovation.
Let $\mathcal F_k$ denote measurements through IMU sample $k$.  Step $k$ uses
only the candidate staged after step $k-1$, processes $y_k$, and then admits
$y_k$ to the tuner, so
\begin{equation}
\vartheta_k\in\mathcal F_{k-1}.
\label{eq:iss-predictable-schedule}
\end{equation}
A candidate formed from $y_k$ first becomes active at $k+1$.  The resulting
one-sample delay is approximately \SI{5}{ms} at \SI{200}{Hz} and supplies the
predictability condition used by the stochastic Kalman argument; the
deterministic UES/ISS result requires only a bounded exogenous schedule.

Assume a Live interval on which
\begin{equation}
0<h_{\min}\le h_k\le h_{\max}<\infty,
\qquad
0<\underline\tau\le\tau(t)\le\overline\tau<\infty,
\label{eq:iss-step-tau-bounds}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
0<\underline\sigma\le\sigma_{aw}(t)\le\overline\sigma<\infty,
\qquad
0<\underline r_S\le r_S(t)\le\overline r_S<\infty,
\label{eq:iss-tuning-bounds}
\end{equation}
and the accelerometer-bias OU correlation time obeys
\begin{equation}
0<\underline\tau_b\le\tau_b\le\overline\tau_b<\infty.
\label{eq:iss-bias-tau-bounds}
\end{equation}
The implementation uses the fixed default $\tau_b=\SI{5000}{s}$; the interval
form also covers a bounded configurable value.

Assume uniformly bounded positive measurement covariances,
\begin{equation}
\underline r\,\mat I
\preceq \mat R_{a,k},\mat R_{m,k},\mat R_{S,k}
\preceq \overline r\,\mat I,
\qquad 0<\underline r\le\overline r<\infty,
\label{eq:iss-R-bounds}
\end{equation}
and positive finite process-noise bounds for gyro noise, gyro-bias random walk,
OU acceleration forcing, and residual accelerometer-bias OU forcing.

Finally assume constants $0<f_0\le f_1$, $0<m_0\le m_1$,
$\delta>0$, and $\overline\omega<\infty$ such that accepted vector updates obey
\begin{equation}
f_0\le\|\widehat{\vct f}_b\|\le f_1,
\qquad
m_0\le\|\widehat{\vct m}_b\|\le m_1,
\label{eq:iss-vector-magnitude-bounds}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
|\vct u_f^\top\vct u_m|\le1-\delta,
\qquad
\|\widehat{\vct\omega}(t)\|\le\overline\omega,
\label{eq:iss-vector-geometry}
\end{equation}
where $\vct u_f$ and $\vct u_m$ are the corresponding unit vectors. Every
finite proof horizon contains four consecutive $S$ pseudo-updates at relative
times
\begin{equation}
0=t_0<t_1<t_2<t_3,
\qquad
T_j:=t_j-t_{j-1}\in[T_-,T_+],
\qquad
0<T_-\le T_+<\infty,
\label{eq:iss-S-gap-bounds}
\end{equation}
and two accepted accelerometer--magnetometer
vector-update instants separated by
$\Delta\in[\Delta_{\min},\Delta_{\max}]$, with
\begin{equation}
\overline\omega\Delta_{\max}<\frac{\pi}{2}.
\label{eq:iss-vector-gap}
\end{equation}
No hard reset occurs inside the horizon.

Equal pseudo-update spacing is unnecessary.  The time-based scheduler serves
each deadline on the first subsequent IMU sample, so realized gaps are
quantized but satisfy
\begin{equation}
T_-\ge h_{\min},
\qquad
T_+\le T_{S,\max}+h_{\max},
\label{eq:iss-scheduler-gap-witness}
\end{equation}
where $T_{S,\max}$ is the largest configured period on the interval.
Lemma~\ref{lem:iss-axis-uco} is proved directly for these unequal gaps.

\subsection{Exact Live-state linearized structure}

With both biases enabled, let $\vct e_{21}$ use the local-error ordering of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:state-vector}.  Here $\vct b_a$ is the stochastic residual in
Eq.~\eqref{eq:acc-bias-temperature-model}; the deterministic temperature mean
is not a Kalman state.

The prediction is block diagonal before covariance coupling through
measurements,
\begin{equation}
\mat F_k=\operatorname{blkdiag}(\mat F_{18,k},\phi_{b,k}\mat I_3),
\qquad
\phi_{b,k}=e^{-h_k/\tau_b}.
\label{eq:iss-F21}
\end{equation}
The 18-state performance block consists of attitude/gyro bias and the three
OU--III translational chains.  Its attitude/gyro-bias transition is
Eq.~\eqref{eq:phi-att-bias} evaluated at $h_k$, and each translational-axis
transition is Eq.~\eqref{eq:phi-axis-ou3} with the corresponding $h_k$ and
$\tau_k$.  The vector-measurement Jacobians are those of
Eqs.~\eqref{eq:acc-meas-jacobians}, \eqref{eq:mag-meas-jacobian}, and
\eqref{eq:HS-selector}.  The optional lever-arm term adds a bounded gyro-bias
Jacobian and does not weaken the argument below; it is omitted from the proof
notation.

\subsection{Uniform observability of the OU--III translational block}

Consider one axis,
\begin{equation}
\dot v=a,\qquad
\dot p=v,\qquad
\dot S=p,\qquad
\dot a=-\lambda(t)a,
\qquad \lambda(t)=1/\tau(t).
\label{eq:iss-scalar-chain}
\end{equation}
Define
\begin{equation}
E(t)=\exp\!\left[-\int_0^t\lambda(s)\,ds\right],
\qquad
A_3(t)=\frac12\int_0^t(t-s)^2E(s)\,ds.
\end{equation}
Then
\begin{equation}
S(t)=S_0+t p_0+\frac{t^2}{2}v_0+A_3(t)a_0.
\end{equation}
At the four pseudo-update instants $t_0,\dots,t_3$ of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-S-gap-bounds}, the observation matrix is
\begin{equation}
\mat O_S=
\begin{bmatrix}
t_0^2/2&t_0&1&A_3(t_0)\\
t_1^2/2&t_1&1&A_3(t_1)\\
t_2^2/2&t_2&1&A_3(t_2)\\
t_3^2/2&t_3&1&A_3(t_3)
\end{bmatrix},
\qquad t_0=0 .
\end{equation}

\begin{lemma}[Uniform observability of one OU--III axis]
\label{lem:iss-axis-uco}
For every measurable $\tau(t)$ satisfying
$\underline\tau\le\tau(t)\le\overline\tau$ and any four consecutive $S$
pseudo-measurements obeying the bounded-gap condition
\eqref{eq:iss-S-gap-bounds},
\begin{equation}
|\det\mat O_S|
\ge T_-^6\exp\!\left(-\frac{3T_+}{\underline\tau}\right)>0.
\label{eq:iss-det-lower}
\end{equation}
Hence the scalar $[v,p,S,a]$ chain is uniformly observable over that
four-update horizon.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Write $f=A_3$ and let $f[t_0,\dots,t_3]$ denote the third divided difference.
For any $f$, the determinant of the matrix with rows
$[1,t_j,t_j^2,f(t_j)]$ equals
$V(t)\,f[t_0,\dots,t_3]$ with the Vandermonde factor
$V(t)=\prod_{i<j}(t_j-t_i)$: both sides are linear in $f$, both annihilate
every polynomial of degree $\le2$, and both equal $V(t)$ at $f(t)=t^3$.
Passing to the column order $[t^2/2,\,t,\,1,\,f]$ transposes the first and
third columns and scales the first by $\tfrac12$, so
\begin{equation}
\det\mat O_S=-\tfrac12\,V(t)\,A_3[t_0,t_1,t_2,t_3].
\label{eq:iss-det-divided-difference}
\end{equation}
Since $A_3'''=E$ is continuous, the Hermite--Genocchi formula gives
\begin{equation}
A_3[t_0,t_1,t_2,t_3]
=\int_{\mathcal T_3}E\!\left(\textstyle\sum_{j}s_jt_j\right)d\mu,
\qquad
\mathcal T_3=\Bigl\{s_j\ge0,\ \textstyle\sum_js_j=1\Bigr\},
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal T_3$ carries total mass $1/3!$. Every argument
$\sum_js_jt_j$ lies in $[0,t_3]$, and $\tau(t)\ge\underline\tau$ gives
$E(u)\ge e^{-u/\underline\tau}$, so
\begin{equation}
A_3[t_0,t_1,t_2,t_3]\ge\tfrac16\,e^{-t_3/\underline\tau}>0 .
\end{equation}
The six pairwise gaps in $V(t)$ are $T_1,T_2,T_3,T_1+T_2,T_2+T_3$ and
$T_1+T_2+T_3$, hence $V(t)\ge12\,T_-^6$, while $t_3\le3T_+$. Substituting both
into \eqref{eq:iss-det-divided-difference} yields
Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-det-lower}. The entries of $\mat O_S$ are uniformly bounded
on the compact parameter set, so nonsingularity with the determinant lower
bound also yields a uniform positive lower bound on
$\sigma_{\min}(\mat O_S)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Reduction to the equal-spacing case]
For $T_1=T_2=T_3=T_S$ one has $V=12T_S^6$ and
$A_3[t_0,\dots,t_3]=\Delta_{T_S}^3A_3(0)/(6T_S^3)$, so
Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-det-divided-difference} collapses to the finite-difference
form $\det\mat O_S=-T_S^3\Delta_{T_S}^3A_3(0)$ and
Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-det-lower} to
$T_S^6\exp(-3T_S/\underline\tau)$. The bounded-gap lemma therefore contains the
exact-cadence statement as a special case rather than replacing it.
\end{remark}

The three-axis 12-state result follows componentwise. Bounded positive
$\mat R_S$ converts this singular-value bound into a uniform finite-horizon
observability-Gramian lower bound.

\subsection{Attitude and gyro-bias finite-horizon observability}

At a vector-update instant define
\begin{equation}
\mat G_k=
\begin{bmatrix}
-[\widehat{\vct f}_{b,k}]_\times\\
-[\widehat{\vct m}_{b,k}]_\times
\end{bmatrix}.
\end{equation}
Using $[x]_\times^\top[x]_\times=\|x\|^2\mat I-xx^\top$ and the noncollinearity
assumption,
\begin{equation}
\mat G_k^\top\mat G_k\succeq\gamma\mat I_3,
\qquad
\gamma>0
\label{eq:iss-G-lower}
\end{equation}
uniformly over accepted updates.

For two accepted vector-update instants separated by $\Delta$, the
attitude/gyro-bias observability matrix contains
\begin{equation}
\mat O_A=
\begin{bmatrix}
\mat G_0&0\\
\mat G_1\mat R_\Delta&\mat G_1\mat B_\Delta
\end{bmatrix}.
\end{equation}
The transverse singular values of
$\mat B_\Delta=\int_0^\Delta e^{-[\omega]_\times s}ds$ are
$2|\sin(\theta/2)|/\|\omega\|$ with $\theta=\|\omega\|\Delta$, while the
rotation-axis singular value is $\Delta$. Condition
Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-vector-gap} therefore gives
\begin{equation}
\sigma_{\min}(\mat B_\Delta)\ge b_0>0.
\end{equation}
If $\mat O_A[\delta\theta^\top,\delta b_g^\top]^\top=0$, the first block and
Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-G-lower} give $\delta\theta=0$, and the second then gives
$\delta b_g=0$. Compactness of the bounded admissible set upgrades full rank to
a uniform singular-value lower bound.

\subsection{The 18-state observable subsystem}

Order the 18-state vector as translation followed by attitude/gyro bias. Select
four $S$ observations and the two vector-update pairs above. The resulting
finite-horizon observation matrix has block-triangular form
\begin{equation}
\mat O_{18}=
\begin{bmatrix}
\mat O_L&0\\
\mat D&\mat O_A
\end{bmatrix},
\end{equation}
where $\mat O_L$ is the three-axis translational matrix and $\mat D$ contains
the legitimate acceleration-to-accelerometer coupling. Since both diagonal
blocks have uniform positive singular-value lower bounds and all blocks are
uniformly bounded, there exists $\alpha_{18}>0$ such that the finite-horizon
Gramian satisfies
\begin{equation}
\mat W_{o,18}(k,N)\succeq\alpha_{18}\mat I_{18}.
\label{eq:iss-uco18}
\end{equation}
Thus the 18-state subsystem is uniformly completely observable.

\subsection{Slow OU bias removes the 21-state obstruction}

For our model,
\begin{equation}
\widetilde{\vct b}_{a,k+1}
=\phi_{b,k}\widetilde{\vct b}_{a,k},
\qquad
\phi_{b,k}=e^{-h_k/\tau_b}.
\end{equation}
By Eqs.~\eqref{eq:iss-step-tau-bounds} and
\eqref{eq:iss-bias-tau-bounds},
\begin{equation}
0<\phi_{b,k}\le
\exp\!\left(-\frac{h_{\min}}{\overline\tau_b}\right)
=:\rho_b<1.
\label{eq:iss-bias-contraction}
\end{equation}
Hence every unobserved residual-bias direction is itself UES.

\begin{lemma}[Uniform detectability of the 21-state augmentation]
\label{lem:iss-21-detectable}
Under the standing assumptions, augmenting the UCO 18-state subsystem by the
slow residual-bias OU state yields a uniformly detectable 21-state LTV system.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Because Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-uco18} holds, there exists a uniformly bounded
observer gain $\mat L_k$ for the 18-state subsystem whose homogeneous observer
error transition is UES:
\begin{equation}
\|\Psi_{18}(k,j)\|\le M_{18}\rho_{18}^{k-j},
\qquad \rho_{18}<1.
\end{equation}
Use the same observer correction for the 18-state coordinates and no
measurement correction for the bias coordinates. The augmented homogeneous
observer error has block-upper-triangular form
\begin{equation}
\begin{bmatrix}
e_{18,k+1}\\ e_{b,k+1}
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
\mat A_{18,k}^{\mathrm{obs}}&-\mat L_k\mat D_k\\
0&\phi_{b,k}\mat I_3
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
e_{18,k}\\ e_{b,k}
\end{bmatrix},
\label{eq:iss-aug-observer}
\end{equation}
where $\mat D_k$ is the bounded measurement coupling of residual bias into the
accelerometer row. The lower block obeys
\begin{equation}
\|e_{b,k}\|\le\rho_b^{k-j}\|e_{b,j}\|.
\end{equation}
The upper off-diagonal contribution is a convolution of two exponentially
decaying sequences,
\begin{equation}
\sum_{\ell=j}^{k-1}
\Psi_{18}(k,\ell+1)\mat L_\ell\mat D_\ell
\Phi_b(\ell,j),
\end{equation}
with uniformly bounded $\mat L_\ell\mat D_\ell$. Such a convolution is bounded
by $C\rho_d^{k-j}$ for some $\rho_d\in(\max\{\rho_{18},\rho_b\},1)$. Thus the
complete observer error is UES, which is the definition of uniform
detectability.
\end{proof}

Uniform observability of the bias coordinates is unnecessary; their intrinsic
contraction is sufficient.  In the random-walk limit $\tau_b\to\infty$,
$\rho_b\to1$ and the uniform detectability margin disappears.

\subsection{Process covariance and Riccati bounds}

For the residual bias OU process, let $\mat Q_{ba}$ be the continuous driving
spectral density retained by the source-compatible
\texttt{set\_Q\_bacc\_rw} API.  Its exact discrete covariance is
Eq.~\eqref{eq:ba-ou-Qd} evaluated at $h=h_k$.  If
\begin{equation}
0<\underline q_b\mat I\preceq\mat Q_{ba}
\preceq\overline q_b\mat I<\infty,
\end{equation}
then compactness of $[h_{\min},h_{\max}]\times
[\underline\tau_b,\overline\tau_b]$ gives finite constants
$0<q_{b,-}\le q_{b,+}<\infty$ with
\begin{equation}
q_{b,-}\mat I\preceq\mat Q_{d,b,k}
\preceq q_{b,+}\mat I.
\end{equation}

The attitude/gyro-bias process covariance has positive gyro and gyro-bias
forcing. Each OU--III translational chain is controllable from its $a_w$ driving
noise over every nonzero interval, so its exact finite-time covariance is
positive definite. The bounded positive lower and upper limits on the driving
spectral densities, $h_k$, and $\tau$ therefore yield
\begin{equation}
0<q_-\mat I\preceq\mat Q_k\preceq q_+\mat I<\infty.
\label{eq:iss-Q-bounds}
\end{equation}

The default periodic $a_w$ covariance maintenance does not invalidate this
Riccati form. It computes
\begin{equation}
\mat\Delta_k=\Pi_+\!\left(
\mat\Sigma_{aw,k}^{\mathrm{stat}}-\mat P_{a_wa_w,k}^-
\right)\succeq0
\end{equation}
and adds it at prediction time,
\begin{equation}
\mat Q_k^{\mathrm{eff}}
=\mat Q_k+\mat E_a\mat\Delta_k\mat E_a^\top.
\end{equation}
Because $\mat\Sigma_{aw}^{\mathrm{stat}}$ is uniformly bounded,
$0\preceq\mat\Delta_k\preceq\overline\Delta\mat I$, and therefore
\begin{equation}
q_-\mat I\preceq\mat Q_k^{\mathrm{eff}}
\preceq(q_++\overline\Delta)\mat I.
\label{eq:iss-Qeff-bounds}
\end{equation}

\begin{theorem}[21-state Live UES]
\label{thm:iss-21-ues}
Under Eqs.~\eqref{eq:iss-step-tau-bounds}--\eqref{eq:iss-vector-gap}, including
the bounded-gap spacing \eqref{eq:iss-S-gap-bounds} of the four $S$
pseudo-updates on each translational
proof horizon, the positive process-density assumptions above, the default PSD
$a_w$ covariance inflation (or no periodic covariance maintenance), active
slow-OU residual accelerometer-bias estimation with finite
$\overline\tau_b$, and no hard reset inside the analyzed Live interval, the
homogeneous 21-state linearized Kalman estimation-error transition is uniformly
exponentially stable. There exist constants $M\ge1$ and $0<\rho<1$,
independent of the admissible exogenous tuning schedule, such that
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
\|\Psi_{21}(k,j)\|\le M\rho^{k-j},\qquad k\ge j .}
\label{eq:iss-21-ues}
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Lemma~\ref{lem:iss-21-detectable} gives uniform detectability. The positive
finite process-covariance bounds in Eqs.~\eqref{eq:iss-Q-bounds}--
\eqref{eq:iss-Qeff-bounds} give uniform stabilizability (indeed the relevant
finite-horizon process Gramians are uniformly positive), and
Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-R-bounds} gives positive finite measurement-covariance bounds.
Standard discrete time-varying Kalman/Riccati theory therefore yields uniform
positive lower and upper covariance bounds
\begin{equation}
0<p_-\mat I\preceq\mat P_k\preceq p_+\mat I<\infty
\end{equation}
and exponential stability of the associated homogeneous Kalman error
transition, which is Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-21-ues}.
\end{proof}

This is a local linearized Live result; it does not claim global EKF convergence
or stability through reset maps.

\subsection{Local 21-state ISS}

Let the nonlinear local error recursion after prediction, measurement update,
and MEKF reset be written
\begin{equation}
\vct e_{k+1}
=\mat A_k^{\mathrm{cl}}\vct e_k
+\vct\varphi_k(\vct e_k)
+\mat G_k\vct d_k,
\label{eq:iss-nonlinear-recursion}
\end{equation}
where the homogeneous transition generated by
$\mat A_k^{\mathrm{cl}}$ satisfies Theorem~\ref{thm:iss-21-ues}, and for some
radius $r_0$ and constant $c_\varphi$,
\begin{equation}
\|\vct\varphi_k(\vct e)\|
\le c_\varphi\|\vct e\|^2,
\qquad \|\vct e\|\le r_0.
\label{eq:iss-quadratic-remainder}
\end{equation}
The disturbance vector $\vct d_k$ collects bounded sensor/model perturbations,
including mismatch of the deterministic temperature calibration, thermal lag,
bias-process mismatch, pseudo-measurement model error, and other bounded
forcing omitted from the nominal homogeneous system.

\begin{theorem}[Local ISS of the 21-state Live error dynamics]
\label{thm:iss-21-local}
Under Theorem~\ref{thm:iss-21-ues}, the quadratic remainder bound
Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-quadratic-remainder}, and a uniform bound
$\|\mat G_k\|\le\overline G$, there exist a sufficiently small invariant
neighborhood, constants $C\ge1$, $0<\lambda<1$, and $\Gamma<\infty$ such that
for every bounded disturbance sequence remaining inside that neighborhood,
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
\|\vct e_k\|
\le C\lambda^{k-j}\|\vct e_j\|
+\Gamma\sup_{j\le i<k}\|\vct d_i\| .}
\label{eq:iss-local-bound}
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Variation of constants and Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-21-ues} give
\begin{align}
\|e_k\|
&\le M\rho^{k-j}\|e_j\|\\
&\quad+M\sum_{i=j}^{k-1}\rho^{k-1-i}
\left(c_\varphi\|e_i\|^2+\overline G\|d_i\|\right).
\end{align}
Choose a ball of radius $r<r_0$ so that the quadratic gain satisfies the usual
small-gain condition, for example
$Mc_\varphi r/(1-\rho)<1$. The quadratic convolution can then be absorbed into
a slightly slower exponential factor $\lambda\in(\rho,1)$, while the bounded
input convolution is at most
$M\overline G(1-\rho)^{-1}\sup_i\|d_i\|$. Shrinking the admissible initial and
input bounds so the trajectory remains in the selected ball closes the local
invariant-set argument and yields Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-local-bound}.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Temperature interpretation}
The state $\vct b_a$ mean-reverts to zero because zero denotes the calibrated
temperature curve, not zero physical bias.  Any error in
$\vct k_a(T-T_{\mathrm{ref}})$ enters $\vct d_k$ and therefore the ISS gain
rather than the homogeneous stability matrix.

\paragraph{Stochastic qualification}
Gaussian process and sensor noise lack an almost-sure finite infinite-horizon
supremum, so Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-local-bound} is a deterministic bounded-input
statement.  Mean-square boundedness or finite-horizon high-probability bounds
are the corresponding stochastic statements for the Gaussian simulation model.

\paragraph{Scope}
The proof covers default prediction-time PSD $a_w$ covariance inflation or
disabled periodic covariance maintenance.  It excludes legacy raw covariance
replacement, startup/re-lock/hard-reset maps, arbitrarily long measurement
outages, collinear gravity/magnetic geometry, and the random-walk limit
$\tau_b=\infty$; a finite upper bound on $\tau_b$ is essential.  Sample-time
quantization and adaptive $S$ pseudo-update deadlines are included because
Lemma~\ref{lem:iss-axis-uco} requires only bounded positive gaps.